00:00:00.890 --> 00:00:03.250
BETH: Every individual
with autism is unique.

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:07.479
They may share traits with others,
but the autism spectrum is wide, and

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:11.469
each individual will have their own
outlook and mix of behaviours, many

00:00:11.469 --> 00:00:13.070
of which can be really positive.

00:00:13.799 --> 00:00:16.619
An individual with autism
can be constantly searching

00:00:16.619 --> 00:00:18.130
for a reason or an answer.

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:22.040
This can help them throughout their
education and into their working life.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.309
JOE: Individuals with autism can have
good concentration levels and may

00:00:26.309 --> 00:00:28.380
focus on a specific activity for hours.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:32.179
They can be thorough and have a
high degree of accuracy in what they

00:00:32.179 --> 00:00:34.319
do with acute attention to detail.

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:38.461
Individuals with autism’s obsessions
and special interests make them inclined

00:00:38.461 --> 00:00:42.623
to thoroughly research things and
find out all they can on a subject.

00:00:42.623 --> 00:00:46.145
BETH: They are mainly visual
learners and get used to being able

00:00:46.145 --> 00:00:48.066
to see things and reproduce them.

00:00:48.066 --> 00:00:52.228
They can spot patterns and repetitions
in things and this can help with

00:00:52.228 --> 00:00:56.900
subjects such as science, maths and
music which all are made up of patterns.

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:03.150
JOE: They may also be good at organising
and categorising, and have good memory for

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:08.389
facts and processes, and once they learn
to do something they can repeat it over

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:10.450
and over again without making mistakes.

00:01:10.799 --> 00:01:13.750
BETH: They can have creative
and distinctive imaginations and

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:17.590
expression of ideas, and a unique
way of thinking outside the box.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:21.639
They are often plain speaking and
honest and may be less likely to judge

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:23.649
others, seeing people for what they are.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:28.179
JOE: Thinking about the positive aspects
of autism can often make life easier

00:01:28.289 --> 00:01:31.880
for a parent and change the opinion
of a teacher or potential employer.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:36.350
It can open doors to new opportunities
and change how individuals with autism

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:40.480
are perceived in the home, school,
employment and in the community.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:44.130
BETH: Rather than focussing on the
weaknesses associated with autism,

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:47.979
people should focus on the strengths
and the positive aspects of autism.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:52.030
A positive approach will help to
build good supportive relationships,

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.420
which can help with improved
development and quality of life.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:00.380
Embracing and celebrating a different
way of thinking can help to improve and

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:04.400
add to the world in general and hopefully
make it a more interesting place.

